      "dom_id": "question-1120",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2}=1$, then $x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2} & =1 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)} & ={7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}=x+1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={7}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & =49 \\\\ x & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
